00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
and we're going to begin in afghanistan  there's less than a week to go before  the deadline to complete evacuations but  the u.s is telling its citizens not to  go to the airport because of a security  threat it's urging americans already  there to leave panics growing among  thousands are still trying to get onto  flights more than 88 000 people have  been flown out of kabul by the us and  allied forces since the taliban's  takeover well let's speak to charlotte  ballas who's in kabul forest just bring  us up to date what the situation is like  at the airport following these warnings  yeah we're getting more threats uh  distributed around isil  different western countries putting out  alerts saying do not come to the airport  because the threat of an isil attack is  quite high the americans did put out a  threat of that nature earlier in the  week nothing has really eventuated after  that the taliban sent in their special  forces to secure the perimeter but still  western nations incredibly worried about  this actually telling their people not  to come to the airport we know about  1500 u.s passport holders remain in  kabul who are meant to be evacuated we  understand that they're starting to do  out of the wire if you will missions  where they they're sending helicopters  to try and retrieve these people and get  them to the airport because of these  threats the taliban also saying to us  you know we're also at threat trying to  secure the perimeter we're also at  threat to isil uh we also understand  that they've got a kind of new strategy  to get people in and we were witnessing  this yesterday where they're putting  people on buses essentially pre-vetting  them at different places around kabul  checking that they've got the paperwork  they need to get through and then taking  those buses as kind of a streamlined  process to get past the taliban that had  been working quite well yesterday but  then we understand last night that the  taliban started stopping some of those  buses on route to the airport and  checking people for documents there  whether or not some people said they  didn't get through after that but we  understand that the us and the taliban  have a deal where the taliban are under  strict instructions not to let anyone  through without a passport without a  green card without verified documents  and there's some confusion about what a  verified document looks like let's take  a quick look at part of the wider  situation in the last few days  particularly we've seen pictures from  people of people in afghanistan  struggling to get money out of atms and  from the banks and of course there is a  wider situation financially as well just  talk us through what the financial  situation looks like over the coming  days for people  i mean if you want to talk about a  crisis point at least in kabul the most  visual crisis point to the west is the  airport but for afghans the biggest  crisis point is money and the economy  and what is going to happen how is the  taliban going to run everything when  many western nations the imf the world  bank are starting to freeze funds on  them i mean as a day-to-day measure  prices of food have started going up  especially in the south uh the banks  have been closed for 10 days they only  opened yesterday but you could only get  out 400  the world bank said yesterday that they  are halting funding for all projects  because they're concerned about the  taliban takeover especially when it  comes to women's rights the problem with  that is that according to a number of  analysts they say that the world bank  actually covers about 30 percent of the  government's budget so that's teachers  salaries that's nurses that's the  taliban says you know world bank funds  run 2 300 small hospitals across  afghanistan if you freeze those funds  because you're concerned about women's  rights what does that actually mean for  people on the ground  who now are struggling to get access to  these type of basic resources  charlotte thank you very much indeed  that's charlotte bellas in kabul  turkeys have begun evacuating its  military personnel from kabul airport  the talibans reportedly asked for  technical help from ankara to run the  airport but it wants its troops out of  the country before the august 31st  deadline turkey has been part of nato's  mission in afghanistan and has hundreds  of troops stationed there  the u.n secretary general says all steps  are being taken to keep operations  running as afghanistan faces a mounting  humanitarian crisis the agency's  airlifted some staff from kabul to  kazakhstan but a temporary office has  been set up mike hanna's good more from  the united nations  the u.n says a third of its  international staff have now been  relocated from afghanistan to its  temporary base in kazakhstan in addition  it says a number of afghan nationals  have also been brought out of the  country though there are no specifics as  to the exact number  the staff have been told to work from  home unless it's necessary for them to  go into un offices to complete their  work  the u.n secretary general held what is  called as a virtual town hall with u.n  staff in the course of the day he is  said to have reassured them that the un  is doing everything it can to protect  their safety but he also says that it's  necessary for the un to carry on its  activities in afghanistan with a view to  the mounting humanitarian crisis  the u.n spokesman confirms that there  will be a substantial staff in country  but would not be drawn on the exact  number that is needed for the un to be  able to continue its operations  all of that is still being worked out  right we are  ongoing with a number of programs i  think we've talked about wfp unicef unh  cr  uh who  obviously as the situation stabilizes uh  we will have a better idea of  what we're able to do what the  humanitarian needs are and what the  staffing is will be that we need to run  those programs and most importantly the  funding that we will need to run those  programs  there have been a number of reports  about discontent among un staff about  the lack of action in terms of  protecting their safety  the un spokesman refuses to comment on  the specific allegations but he did  confirm that a number of letters have  been received from various unions which  are being considered by the secretariat

